"EMK" <noneofyourbusin...@columbus.rr.com> wrote:
>
> Since I've been struggling to get a character past Exp Lvl 10 I've been
> reading some tips online. =A0There are some that suggest that you stay on
> D-lvl 1 until you hit E-lvl 3

My preference is to be at XP=3D2 before descending to level 2, XP=3D3
before descending to level 3.  This pattern makes some amount of
sense up to XP=3D5 as long as you aren't avoiding XP levels for some
reason.  The pattern rapidly breaks down within a few levels.

> ... Others suggest letting your pet kill everything on the
> first few levels to get up its hit points.

Strong pets are very helpful.

> I'm not sure if these are mutually exclusive strategies,

Consider that pets attack anything below some level of meanness
and that level tracks their own current meanness.  Pets use a
simple strategy that doesn't work for players - When injured to
half their hit points they break combat and retreat to heal.  it
only works because monsters don't swarm pets and don't attack
pets except in defense.

So if you kill the stronger monsters using smarter strategies and
let your pet kill weaker monsters it does teamwork just fine.

> I guess overall I have the
> impression that there aren't enough monsters re-spawning on a level I've
> cleared to warrant me sticking around very long.

It's a trade off.  The meanness of monsters grows sort of linear
with depth.  The points needed to get new XP levels grows sort
of expoential with depth.  Early on it's easy to level; later on it's
hard to level just by combat experience.  This seems to be a
deliberate feature of the game.

> I'm probably mistaken
> about that, but that's just the feeling I've gotten so far. =A0On average=
 I
> find that I hit the E-lvl of a D-lvl shortly after I've started on that
> level, playing what I consider "comfortably".

I end up nearing XP=3D9 by the bottom of the mines.  By that time
the correlation of XP and depth to be gained by combat gets
more and more unlinked.

> So, what would you veteran players suggest? =A0Should I just suck it up a=
nd
> stick to a formula of not hitting the next D-lvl until I'm a E-lvl or two
> ahead of it, or will I find that a strategy like that will just leave me
> with a pile of starved corpses?

It works okay XP=3D1-5.  It is to be ignored almost completely XP=3D15+.
In between, it's far more a matter of context.

Note that all of my post assumes you aren't avoiding levels for some
reason.  There are good reasons for avoiding levels that invalidate my
suggestions here.


